{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1217 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "5e78442bbf6195d40d2cb058b96d23256b68cebb2d770b25ed7cdbb7216fef91" :edb/db-before-t 1216 :edb/db-after-t 1217 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534529 50 #inst "2026-10-12T02:03:30.840Z" 13194139534529 17592186045417 true] [17592186157102 1074 "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} f(x,y) \\ \\mathrm d A = \\iint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\ f(r\\cos\\theta, r\\sin\\theta) \\:  r \\ \\mathrm d r \\mathrm d \\theta.\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $1$ centered at $(-1,0).$ \n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's write our curves in polar coordinates.\n\n- For $(x+1)^2+y^2=1,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+1)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +1)^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2} & =1 \\\\ {r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +1+{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )+2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}+2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r = -2\\cos\\theta.$\n\n- For $x=-1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-1 \\\\ r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =-1 \\\\ r & =-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } \\\\ r & =-\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's find the limits for the parameter $\\theta.$ Our curves intersect at the point $(-1,1).$ So, the entire region $D$ lies between the polar lines $\\theta=\\dfrac{3\\pi}{4}$ and $\\theta=\\pi,$ as depicted below.  \n\n![](images/76/769c13bea2dfc91aa3a507df1b7a763ae0da7591e0d6ba5face12268efb88240.png)\n\nAs a result, the region can be expressed in polar coordinates $(r, \\theta)$ as \n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\Big\\{ (r,\\theta) \\: : \\: -\\sec\\theta \\leq r \\leq -2\\cos\\theta, \\: \\dfrac{3\\pi}{4} \\leq \\theta \\leq {\\pi} \\Big\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}xy\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3\\pi /4}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }^{-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3\\pi /4}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{-\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }^{-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534529 17592186045417 true] [17592186157102 1074 "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} f(x,y) \\ \\mathrm d A = \\iint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\ f(r\\cos\\theta, r\\sin\\theta) \\:  r \\ \\mathrm d r \\mathrm d \\theta.\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $1$ centered at $(-1,0).$ \n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's write our curves in polar coordinates.\n\n- For $(x+1)^2+y^2=1,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+1)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +1)^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2} & =1 \\\\ {r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +1+{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )+2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}+2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r = -2\\cos\\theta.$\n\n- For $x=-1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-1 \\\\ r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =-1 \\\\ r & =-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } \\\\ r & =-\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's find the limits for the parameter $\\theta.$ Our curves intersect at the point $(-1,1).$ So, the entire region $D$ lies between the polar lines $\\theta=\\dfrac{3\\pi}{4}$ and $\\theta=\\pi,$ as depicted below.  \n\n![](images/76/769c13bea2dfc91aa3a507df1b7a763ae0da7591e0d6ba5face12268efb88240.png)\n\nAs a result, the region can be expressed in polar coordinates $(r, \\theta)$ as \n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\Big\\{ (r,\\theta) \\: : \\: -\\sec\\theta \\leq r \\leq -2\\cos\\theta, \\: \\dfrac{3\\pi}{4} \\leq \\theta \\leq {\\pi} \\Big\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}xy\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3\\pi /4}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }^{-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3\\pi /4}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{-\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }^{-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534529 17592186045417 false] [17592186157103 1074 "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} f(x,y) \\ \\mathrm d A = \\iint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\ f(r\\cos\\theta, r\\sin\\theta) \\:  r \\ \\mathrm d r \\mathrm d \\theta.\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $\\dfrac{1}{2}$ centered at $\\left(0,\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)\\!.$ \n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's write our curves in polar coordinates.\n\n- For $x^2+\\left( y-\\dfrac{1}{2} \\right)^2=\\dfrac14,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{(y-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+{(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ {r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta -r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\frac{1}{4} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )-r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}-r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r = \\sin\\theta.$\n\n- For $y=\\dfrac{1}{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ r & =\\frac{1}{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\ r & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's find the limits for the parameter $\\theta.$ Our curves intersect at the point $\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2},\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right).$ So, the entire region lies between the radial lines $\\theta=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$ and $\\theta=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2},$ as depicted below.  \n\n![](images/12/1284a8b4b3f1634b4f7586d40ab8adf9cf93c69484b7ed069f138c8055e88892.png)\n\nAs a result, the region can be expressed in polar coordinates $(r, \\theta)$ as \n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\Big\\{ (r,\\theta) \\: : \\: \\dfrac{1}{2}\\csc\\theta \\leq r \\leq \\sin\\theta, \\: \\dfrac{\\pi}{4} \\leq \\theta \\leq \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\Big\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{2y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{1/2\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{1/2\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta [r]_{1/2\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}(2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta -1)\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )]_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}(0-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534529 17592186045417 true] [17592186157103 1074 "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} f(x,y) \\ \\mathrm d A = \\iint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\ f(r\\cos\\theta, r\\sin\\theta) \\:  r \\ \\mathrm d r \\mathrm d \\theta.\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $\\dfrac{1}{2}$ centered at $\\left(0,\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)\\!.$ \n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's write our curves in polar coordinates.\n\n- For $x^2+\\left( y-\\dfrac{1}{2} \\right)^2=\\dfrac14,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{(y-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+{(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ {r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta -r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\frac{1}{4} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )-r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}-r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r = \\sin\\theta.$\n\n- For $y=\\dfrac{1}{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ r & =\\frac{1}{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\ r & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's find the limits for the parameter $\\theta.$ Our curves intersect at the point $\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2},\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right).$ So, the entire region lies between the radial lines $\\theta=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$ and $\\theta=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2},$ as depicted below.  \n\n![](images/12/1284a8b4b3f1634b4f7586d40ab8adf9cf93c69484b7ed069f138c8055e88892.png)\n\nAs a result, the region can be expressed in polar coordinates $(r, \\theta)$ as \n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\Big\\{ (r,\\theta) \\: : \\: \\dfrac{1}{2}\\csc\\theta \\leq r \\leq \\sin\\theta, \\: \\dfrac{\\pi}{4} \\leq \\theta \\leq \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\Big\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{2y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{1/2\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{1/2\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta [r]_{1/2\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}(2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta -1)\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )]_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}(0-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534529 17592186045417 false] [17592186157298 1038 #edb/ref 17592186424328 13194139534529 17592186045417 true] [17592186157298 1038 #edb/ref 17592186424329 13194139534529 17592186045417 true] [17592186157299 1038 #edb/ref 17592186424327 13194139534529 17592186045417 true] [17592186157300 1038 #edb/ref 17592186424330 13194139534529 17592186045417 true] [17592186157300 1038 #edb/ref 17592186424331 13194139534529 17592186045417 true] [17592186157490 1050 900.0 13194139534529 17592186045417 true] [17592186158486 1064 "Suppose we want to compute the double integral \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_{D}{{\\sqrt{x^2 + y^2}\\,\\mathrm d A}},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $D$ is a washer enclosed between two circles centered at $(0,0)$ with radii $1$ and $2,$ respectively, as shown below.\n\n![](images/47/4752982aaf806b9e3b9c4d28d7a92bd01c3ff6a68afcca4235061a01b2fbc86f.png)\n\nThe easiest way to solve this problem is to convert it to polar coordinates. Let's define a transformation $\\mathbf T$ as\n\n$$\nx=r\\cos\\theta, \\qquad y = r\\sin\\theta.\n$$\n\nThe preimage of $D$ under the action of $\\mathbf T$ is the rectangular region $\\Delta,$ given by \n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\big\\{ (r,\\theta) \\: : \\: 1 \\leq r \\leq 2, \\: 0 \\leq \\theta \\leq 2\\pi \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and its preimage $\\Delta$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/e0/e0cc6c7f92704365c3ce15b91d85f703330b767a01f1f773b014cf61620563a3.png)\n\n             \n\nThe change of variables formula for polar coordinates states \n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} f(x,y) \\ \\mathrm d A = \\iint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\ f(r\\cos\\theta, r\\sin\\theta) \\:  r \\ \\mathrm d r \\mathrm d \\theta.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,dA & ={∬}_{Δ}\\sqrt{(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}}\\cdot r\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{2}{r}^{2}\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating this integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{2}{r}^{2}\\,dr\\,d\\theta  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }d\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{2}\\,dr \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{2}{r}^{2}\\,dr \\\\  & =2\\pi \\cdot {[\\frac{1}{3}{r}^{3}]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\pi \\cdot [{2}^{3}-{1}^{3}] \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\pi \\cdot 7 \\\\  & =\\frac{14\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D}{{\\sqrt{x^2 + y^2}\\,\\mathrm d A}} = \\dfrac{14\\pi}{3}.\n$$" 13194139534529 17592186045417 true] [17592186158486 1064 "Suppose we want to compute the double integral \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_{D}{{\\sqrt{x^2 + y^2}\\,\\mathrm d A}},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $D$ is a washer enclosed between two circles centered at $(0,0)$ with radii $1$ and $2,$ respectively, as shown below.\n\n![](images/47/4752982aaf806b9e3b9c4d28d7a92bd01c3ff6a68afcca4235061a01b2fbc86f.png)\n\nThe easiest way to solve this problem is to convert it to polar coordinates. Let's define a transformation $\\mathbf T$ as\n\n$$\nx=r\\cos\\theta, \\qquad y = r\\sin\\theta.\n$$\n\nThe preimage of $D$ under the action of $\\mathbf T$ is the rectangular region $\\Delta,$ given by \n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\big\\{ (r,\\theta) \\: : \\: 1 \\leq r \\leq 2, \\: 0 \\leq \\theta \\leq 2\\pi \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and its preimage $\\Delta$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/e0/e0cc6c7f92704365c3ce15b91d85f703330b767a01f1f773b014cf61620563a3.png)\n\nThe change of variables formula for polar coordinates states \n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} f(x,y) \\ \\mathrm d A = \\iint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\ f(r\\cos\\theta, r\\sin\\theta) \\:  r \\ \\mathrm d r \\mathrm d \\theta.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,dA & ={∬}_{Δ}\\sqrt{(r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )^{2}}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}({cos}^{2}\\theta +{sin}^{2}\\theta )}\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}}\\cdot r\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{2}{r}^{2}\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating this integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{2}{r}^{2}\\,dr\\,d\\theta  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }d\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{2}\\,dr \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{2}{r}^{2}\\,dr \\\\  & =2\\pi \\cdot {[\\frac{1}{3}{r}^{3}]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\pi \\cdot [{2}^{3}-{1}^{3}] \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\pi \\cdot 7 \\\\  & =\\frac{14\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D}{{\\sqrt{x^2 + y^2}\\,\\mathrm d A}} = \\dfrac{14\\pi}{3}.\n$$" 13194139534529 17592186045417 false] [17592186158487 1064 "Let's get some more practice at expressing regions in polar coordinates. \n\nConsider the region enclosed by the curve $x^2+(y-2)^2=4$ and the line $y=2,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/df/dffcd94cc96c33d173704a63cb03fb0f0098bc67d6c66a855c16f59694bde8e1.png)\n\nThis region represents a portion of the disk of radius $2$ centered at $(0,2).$\n\nFirst, we recall the usual transformations from polar to Cartesian coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now use the above formulas to write the functions that bound our region in polar coordinates:\n\n- For $x^2+(y-2)^2 = 4,$  we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+(y-2)^{2} & =4 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -2)^{2} & =4 \\\\ ({r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta -4r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +4 & =4 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )-4r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}-4r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r = 4\\sin\\theta.$\n\n- For $y=2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2 \\\\ r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =2 \\\\ r & =\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\ r & =2\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's determine the limits for $r$ and $\\theta.$ \n\nFirst of all, note that if we draw an arrow through the region from the origin, it enters the region via the curve $r=2\\csc\\theta$ and exits via $r=4\\sin\\theta \\mathbin{:}$  \n\n![](images/b0/b09ab4e4e08dfb056964947dc866b965940b761d70c945faf001446c1df7869f.png)\n\nSecondly, our curves intersect at the points $(-2,2)$ and $(2,2).$ So, the entire region $D$ lies between the polar lines $\\theta=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$ and $\\theta=\\dfrac{3\\pi}{4},$ as depicted below.  \n\n![](images/6a/6a132d5f8c58ce60c794595ee80c57c5b9b02e630004808cd53a7677efd022e7.png)\n\nTherefore, in polar coordinates $(r, \\theta),$ the regi