 true] [17592186424325 1075 #edb/ref 17592186424315 13194139534529 17592186045417 true] [17592186424325 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139534529 17592186045417 true] [17592186424325 1077 "selection" 13194139534529 17592186045417 true] [17592186424325 1078 #edb/ref 17592186424307 13194139534529 17592186045417 true] [17592186424325 1079 #uuid "daf8a023-f586-55e1-889c-e57ed171c509" 13194139534529 17592186045417 true] [17592186424326 1075 #edb/ref 17592186424297 13194139534529 17592186045417 true] [17592186424326 1075 #edb/ref 17592186424302 13194139534529 17592186045417 true] [17592186424326 1075 #edb/ref 17592186424305 13194139534529 17592186045417 true] [17592186424326 1075 #edb/ref 17592186424309 13194139534529 17592186045417 true] [17592186424326 1075 #edb/ref 17592186424317 13194139534529 17592186045417 true] [17592186424326 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139534529 17592186045417 true] [17592186424326 1077 "selection" 13194139534529 17592186045417 true] [17592186424326 1078 #edb/ref 17592186424297 13194139534529 17592186045417 true] [17592186424326 1079 #uuid "dc66dd31-0a57-5231-b848-a0871bc42f3a" 13194139534529 17592186045417 true] [17592186424327 1068 #edb/ref 17592186424323 13194139534529 17592186045417 true] [17592186424327 1069 "q-161103" 13194139534529 17592186045417 true] [17592186424327 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534529 17592186045417 true] [17592186424327 1071 "![](images/a5/a56bcec93b5cc66ef8238b7fe7b213c5e184f1cf4b11baf4524525a63a284780.png)\n\nEvaluate the double integral ${∬}_{D}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A,$ where the region $D$ is enclosed between two circles centered at $O$ of radii $1$ and $3,$ as shown above." 13194139534529 17592186045417 true] [17592186424327 1072 #uuid "45cc9fce-954c-547e-835b-7dec4ec6d636" 13194139534529 17592186045417 true] [17592186424327 1074 "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents the annulus between the disks of radii $1$ and $3$ centered at the origin. \n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write our curves in polar coordinates.\n\n- For ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1,$ we obtain $r=1.$\n\n- For ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=9,$ we obtain $r=3.$\n\nAs a result, the region can be expressed in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$ as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,1\\le r\\le 3,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\sqrt{(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{3}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}}\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{3}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{[\\frac{{r}^{3}}{3}]}_{1}^{3}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(9-\\frac{1}{3})\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{26}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{26}{3}[\\theta ]_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{26}{3}\\cdot 2\\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{52\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534529 17592186045417 true] [17592186424328 1068 #edb/ref 17592186424326 13194139534529 17592186045417 true] [17592186424328 1069 "q-85181" 13194139534529 17592186045417 true] [17592186424328 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534529 17592186045417 true] [17592186424328 1071 "![](images/3d/3d5542933934102149c58153c3108d55d9c615e991db8aca8d8fc1e60837917f.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is enclosed between the curve ${x}^{2}+(y-2)^{2}=4$ and the line $y=3,$ as shown above. What is\n${∬}_{D}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A$\nexpressed in polar coordinates?" 13194139534529 17592186045417 true] [17592186424328 1072 #uuid "78459587-308f-5867-9175-f728a41ccebc" 13194139534529 17592186045417 true] [17592186424328 1074 "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $2$ centered at $(0,2).$ \n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write our curves in polar coordinates.\n\n- For ${x}^{2}+(y-2)^{2}=4,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+(y-2)^{2} & =4 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -2)^{2} & =4 \\\\ {r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta -4r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +4 & =4 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )-4r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}-4r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$\n\n- For $y=3,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =3 \\\\ r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =3 \\\\ r & =\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\ r & =3\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's find the limits for the parameter $\\theta .$ Our curves intersect at the point $(-\\sqrt{3},3).$ So, the entire region $D$ lies between the polar lines $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{2\\pi }{3},$ as depicted below.  \n\n![](images/49/4903ca2ba8cde3f7874e10998a0741035ec7b2dea69a7804eaca64cb552fc572.png)\n\nAs a result, the region can be expressed in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$ as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,3\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta \\le r\\le 4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}}{(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi /3}{\\int }_{3\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }^{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\frac{{r}^{3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{{r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi /3}{\\int }_{3\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }^{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }r{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi /3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{3\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }^{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534529 17592186045417 true] [17592186424329 1068 #edb/ref 17592186424322 13194139534529 17592186045417 true] [17592186424329 1069 "q-83979" 13194139534529 17592186045417 true] [17592186424329 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534529 17592186045417 true] [17592186424329 1071 "![](images/8a/8a51719e208dafaa32d7f28c338885313942513493bd9fe0392b1ef06004e91e.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is enclosed between the curve $(x-1)^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ and the line $x=1,$ as shown above. What is\n${∬}_{D}(x+y)\\,dA$\nexpressed in polar coordinates?" 13194139534529 17592186045417 true] [17592186424329 1072 #uuid "97691a5c-96a1-5dc1-b545-4fdb3272878f" 13194139534529 17592186045417 true] [17592186424329 1074 "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $1$ centered at $(1,0).$ \n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write our curves in polar coordinates.\n\n- For $(x-1)^{2}+{y}^{2}=1,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-1)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -1)^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2} & =1 \\\\ {r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +1+{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )-2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}-2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$\n\n- For $x=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =1 \\\\ r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ r & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } \\\\ r & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's find the limits for the parameter $\\theta .$ Our curves intersect at the points $(1,-1)$ and $(1,1).$ So, the entire region $D$ lies between the polar lines $\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{4},$ as depicted below.  \n\n![](images/43/4371a691b81b2ed1cb5d3468f68cd24f41e8a6b87d378a3206238d58e61791f0.png)\n\nAs a result, the region can be expressed in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$ as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta \\le r\\le 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}x+y\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }^{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{r}^{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ){\\int }_{\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }^{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534529 17592186045417 true] [17592186424330 1068 #edb/ref 17592186424324 13194139534529 17592186045417 true] [17592186424330 1069 "q-85175" 13194139534529 17592186045417 true] [17592186424330 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534529 17592186045417 true] [17592186424330 1071 "![](images/44/44b6b5826b5d8532fef3a9c8e56a03b3244a4aaa33ae00b4b12b69364c51f001.png)\n\nThe region $D$ in the right-half plane is enclosed between two semi-circles centered at $O$ of radii $1$ and $4,$ as shown above. What is \n${∬}_{D}\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A$\nexpressed in polar coordinates?" 13194139534529 17592186045417 true] [17592186424330 1072 #uuid "a0b5051e-fba0-51c4-bd0d-e4424f08ff88" 13194139534529 17592186045417 true] [17592186424330 1074 "We will use the change of variables formula in the form \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ represents a portion of the washer enclosed between the circles of radii $1$ and $4$ centered at the origin. In polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,1\\le r\\le 4,\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{{r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534529 17592186045417 true] [17592186424331 1068 #edb/ref 17592186424325 13194139534529 17592186045417 true] [17592186424331 1069 "q-83946" 13194139534529 17592186045417 true] [17592186424331 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139534529 17592186045417 true] [17592186424331 1071 "![](images/31/31054ef4614814dd1c9131192504ea37f3527af75d8292fbce943c16235c9303.png)\n\nThe region $D$ in the second quadrant is enclosed between two quarter-circles centered at $O$ of radii $1$ and $3,$ as shown above. What is\n${∬}_{D}(x+y)\\,\\text{d}A$ \nexpressed in polar coordinates?" 13194139534529 17592186045417 true] [17592186424331 1072 #uuid "ff6bf6f1-3574-59b2-8046-e358528c7f1f" 13194139534529 17592186045417 true] [17592186424331 1074 "We will use the change of variables formula in the form \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ represents a portion of the washer enclosed between the circles of radii $1$ and $3$ centered at the origin. In polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,1\\le r\\le 3,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(x+y)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{3}(r(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ))\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{3}{r}^{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534529 17592186045417 true]] :edb/tempids {"answer-055b0e6b-d664-5538-b782-3dfbe82f8ee7" 17592186424297 "answer-14619b02-c3bb-5be6-85af-30af1494c733" 17592186424298 "answer-164a2e88-b870-534a-838f-cf3c412c7a85" 17592186424299 "answer-21488b88-2acf-5a8b-8507-da4310f1130f" 17592186424300 "answer-24cfcda8-afbc-5331-885b-bcfa05c0d741" 17592186424301 "answer-320a2c42-e7e8-5a89-b8ed-772fad2d8845" 17592186424302 "answer-441c7356-d182-50d5-a1bd-8b54901c3f5a" 17592186424303 "answer-466e97c1-fc77-5fb7-94db-decc56abd8f9" 17592186424304 "answer-4e20bf88-0220-51b3-8e06-d32a2b7d8220" 17592186424305 "answer-54526e04-2273-5cf8-9d70-fdb80efe57d2" 17592186424306 "answer-5553ca7e-bc04-53a9-ab03-d33a73524410" 17592186424307 "answer-66e1a5e9-95e1-574c-a839-41136951a82e" 17592186424308 "answer-776ef258-4be4-56d1-84fa-73c7f0f2fd04" 17592186424309 "answer-778dd242-bc8c-5163-ba5e-b482fc9a6724" 17592186424310 "answer-7f18edf8-e5d2-5da6-9302-024ff289f750" 17592186424311 "answer-84775023-9e2b-50b6-8361-4921f16d83bc" 17592186424312 "answer-91bb664e-dbb4-5152-b157-eaf96ebfee48" 17592186424313 "answer-9bdc8ff4-a08f-5057-bc05-25918e661b16" 17592186424314 "answer-aa4cbdd3-75cd-5bc5-8f98-2159597bf317" 17592186424315 "answer-ac4c807c-3751-5c8a-ba9f-d367e6a9e011" 17592186424316 "answer-cd6a190c-ce1c-504a-9a43-1fa603eb0266" 17592186424317 "answer-e203c470-87b8-5386-8b7b-5e7971c12cc4" 17592186424318 "answer-f154bcd4-8668-5f01-afce-656a9407bcc6" 17592186424319 "answer-f1a5a807-5f29-57e4-ac91-cb6041b0cd30" 17592186424320 "answer-f40e93e6-995f-501f-964e-89e8ceda688e" 17592186424321 "answer-field-4f3ffa13-5a50-5681-afc0-a669c98e966e" 17592186424322 "answer-field-64089c5c-af77-5ef9-b56b-0b6dff4fd9ce" 17592186424323 "answer-field-a78d9d7e-8041-59b2-a1b7-9e4905f4e6d8" 17592186424324 "answe